import{_ as t,o,c as d,ag as a}from"./chunks/framework.D1z9wg1y.js";const p=JSON.parse('{"title":"Adapting the profile","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"profile.md","filePath":"profile.md"}'),l={name:"profile.md"};function s(n,e,c,i,r,h){return o(),d("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[a('<h1 id="adapting-the-profile" tabindex="-1">Adapting the profile <a class="header-anchor" href="#adapting-the-profile" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Adapting the profile&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Four private files adapt the harness to one user and one machine. None of them is in the repository. They are in <code>~/.config/research-harness/</code>, and <code>examples/</code> holds an example of each, with a comment on every key that says what its value is.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;">file</th><th style="text-align:left;">what it holds</th><th style="text-align:left;">required</th><th style="text-align:left;">its path</th><th style="text-align:left;">read by</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>profile.toml</code>, the <a href="./concepts#profile">profile</a></td><td style="text-align:left;">every value that names a person, an institution or a machine</td><td style="text-align:left;">yes</td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>harness --profile F</code>, otherwise <code>$RESEARCH_HARNESS_PROFILE</code>, otherwise <code>~/.config/research-harness/profile.toml</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the verbs that <a href="#the-keys-of-the-profile">The keys of the profile</a> names for each key</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>models.toml</code>, the model tables</td><td style="text-align:left;">the model of each <a href="./concepts#tier">tier</a> for each <a href="./concepts#frontend">frontend</a></td><td style="text-align:left;">for <code>install</code></td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>harness --models F</code>, otherwise <code>$RESEARCH_HARNESS_MODELS</code>, otherwise beside the profile</td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>install</code> only</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><a href="#the-providers"><code>providers.toml</code></a></td><td style="text-align:left;">the model servers and accounts of OpenCode and oh-my-pi</td><td style="text-align:left;">no</td><td style="text-align:left;">beside the profile</td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>install</code> only</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><a href="#the-network-overlay"><code>network.toml</code></a>, the network overlay</td><td style="text-align:left;">your own hosts for the network lists of Claude Code</td><td style="text-align:left;">no</td><td style="text-align:left;">beside the profile</td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>install</code>, <code>render</code> of the settings template, <code>settings</code></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Copy each example that you need and write your values into the copy, as the step <em>The profile and the model tables</em> of <a href="./setup-macos#the-profile-and-the-model-tables">setup-macos.md</a> and <a href="./setup-linux#the-profile-and-the-model-tables">setup-linux.md</a> shows. A template of the repository names a profile key as <code>{key}</code>, and a verb renders the template with the profile before it uses it. A template that names a key that the profile does not have stops the verb, and the message names the key.</p><h2 id="read-a-value" tabindex="-1">Read a value <a class="header-anchor" href="#read-a-value" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Read a value&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>harness get</code> prints the value of one key, one line for each item of a list. <code>harness render</code> prints a template rendered with the profile. Run these commands in the checkout:</p><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">harness</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> get</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> repository_roots</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">harness</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> render</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> launchagents/kaimon.plist</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Both verbs read every key that they name.</p><h2 id="the-home-directory-and" tabindex="-1">The home directory and <code>~</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-home-directory-and" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The home directory and `~`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The key <code>home</code> is optional. Without it, <code>home</code> is the home directory of the user who runs <code>harness</code>. A given <code>home</code> must be an absolute path without <code>~</code>; otherwise the verb stops, and the message names the key. A trailing slash is removed.</p><p>A string value that is <code>~</code>, or that starts with <code>~/</code>, means <code>home</code>: <code>~</code> becomes <code>home</code>, and <code>~/Research</code> becomes <code>home</code> followed by <code>/Research</code>. The same holds for each such item of a list. Any other <code>~</code> stays as written, <code>a~b</code> and <code>~name/x</code> too. So <code>examples/profile.toml</code> works as copied, on every machine.</p><h2 id="one-profile-for-every-machine" tabindex="-1">One profile for every machine <a class="header-anchor" href="#one-profile-for-every-machine" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;One profile for every machine&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The template host is the machine whose Claude Code memory is the template for the other machines. The key <code>autocommit_branch</code> names the branch of the <code>origin</code> of <code>~/.claude</code> that <code>claude-autocommit</code> pushes to: <code>main</code> on the template host, and a branch of its own on each other machine. The optional key <code>template_host</code> names the template host by its short host name: the host name up to its first <code>.</code>, as <code>hostname -s</code> prints it. Without <code>autocommit_branch</code> in the profile, its value is:</p><ul><li><code>main</code> when the profile has no <code>template_host</code>;</li><li><code>main</code> on the machine whose short host name is <code>template_host</code>;</li><li><code>memory/&lt;short host name&gt;</code> on every other machine.</li></ul><p>The comparison is exact, so <code>Alpha</code> and <code>alpha</code> are two machines, and an empty host name gives <code>main</code>. A given <code>autocommit_branch</code> is the branch on every machine. A <code>template_host</code> that is empty, holds a <code>.</code> or is not a string stops the verb, and the message names the key. Every verb reads the same value, and <code>harness get autocommit_branch</code> prints it. So with <code>template_host</code> and no <code>autocommit_branch</code>, one profile works unedited on every machine of one user: write it once, keep it in a private place, and copy it to each machine.</p><h2 id="the-keys-of-the-profile" tabindex="-1">The keys of the profile <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-keys-of-the-profile" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The keys of the profile&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The table names each key of <code>examples/profile.toml</code> and the verbs that read it, in addition to <code>harness get</code> and <code>harness render</code>.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;">key</th><th style="text-align:left;">what it holds</th><th style="text-align:left;">read by</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>home</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the home directory, an absolute path. Optional: without it, the home directory of the user who runs <code>harness</code></td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>install</code> and <code>settings</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>harness</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the path of the checkout of this repository</td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>install</code>, in the OpenCode configuration</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>memory_project</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">a label, not a path: the settings set <code>autoMemoryDirectory</code> to <code>~/.claude/projects/&lt;memory_project&gt;/memory</code>, so Claude Code keeps the tree&#39;s memory there, whatever the tree&#39;s path is. The same value on every machine that shares one memory pool, such as Claude Code&#39;s name of the tree&#39;s project on the first machine (its path with each <code>/</code> replaced by <code>-</code>); it need not match the path on another machine</td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>install</code> and <code>settings</code>, in the settings template</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>tree_agents</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the directory of the <a href="./concepts#tree-instructions">tree instructions</a></td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>install</code>, which <a href="./concepts#install">installs</a> them into <code>~/.claude/</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>launchd_prefix</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the reverse-domain prefix of the labels of the launchd jobs in <code>launchagents/</code>, such as <code>org.example</code>: the label of the Kaimon server is <code>&lt;launchd_prefix&gt;.kaimon</code>. macOS only; nothing on Linux reads it</td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>render</code> of the <code>launchagents/</code> templates; <code>doctor</code>; the update job <code>scripts/julia-update.jl</code> on macOS</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>template_host</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the short host name of the template host. Optional</td><td style="text-align:left;">every verb that reads the profile, for the value of <code>autocommit_branch</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>autocommit_branch</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the branch of the <code>origin</code> of <code>~/.claude</code> that <code>claude-autocommit</code> pushes to: <code>main</code> on the template host, a branch of its own on each other machine. Optional: without it, <code>main</code> on the machine of <code>template_host</code> or without <code>template_host</code>, otherwise <code>memory/&lt;short host name&gt;</code>, as <a href="#one-profile-for-every-machine">One profile for every machine</a> says</td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>render</code> of <code>launchagents/claude-autocommit.plist</code> and <code>systemd/claude-autocommit.service</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>org</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the GitHub accounts and organisations where a <a href="./concepts#session">session</a> can open and comment on a pull request or an issue with no prompt</td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>install</code> and <code>settings</code>, in the settings template; <code>leaks</code>, which also searches for each of them</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>repository_roots</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the directories that hold the repositories of the research tree</td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>leaks</code>, which searches for the name of each directory below them</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>docs_exceptions</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the repositories that keep a documentation workflow of their own</td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>workflows</code>; <code>ci-protection</code>, through <code>githooks/verify-workflows.jl</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>docs_additions</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the repositories that add steps to the canonical documentation workflow</td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>workflows</code>; <code>ci-protection</code>, through <code>githooks/verify-workflows.jl</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>protected_dir</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the directories below <code>home</code> that no session can edit, each as a path relative to <code>home</code>, such as <code>Nextcloud</code></td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>install</code>, in the settings template and the OpenCode configuration; <code>settings</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>leak</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the strings that must never appear in the repository, such as your user name and the hosts of your institution. The item <code>~</code> stands for the path of your home directory, as each <code>~</code> of the profile does</td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>leaks</code></td></tr></tbody></table><p><code>install</code> reads the settings template too, because it writes the permission layer of oh-my-pi from it. So each key of the settings template is a key that <code>install</code> reads.</p><p><code>leaks</code> also renders every template of the repository, but with <code>examples/profile.toml</code>, the home <code>/home/example</code> and the host name <code>example-other</code>, not with your profile. From your profile it reads only <code>leak</code>, <code>org</code> and <code>repository_roots</code>.</p><h2 id="the-tables-of-the-model-tables" tabindex="-1">The tables of the model tables <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-tables-of-the-model-tables" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The tables of the model tables&quot;">​</a></h2><p>An <a href="./concepts#agent">agent</a> or a <a href="./concepts#skill">skill</a> names a tier in its <code>model:</code>: <code>large</code>, <code>medium</code> or <code>small</code>. Only <code>harness install</code> reads the model tables. It maps each tier to the model of each frontend.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;">table</th><th style="text-align:left;">what it holds</th><th style="text-align:left;">read by</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>[claude]</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the Claude Code model of each tier, written as the <code>model:</code> of the installed agent or skill; all three tiers are required</td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>install</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>[models]</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the OpenCode and oh-my-pi model of each tier, as <code>provider/model</code>; the <code>medium</code> model is also oh-my-pi&#39;s default model, in <code>harness.yml</code>, and oh-my-pi needs all three tiers</td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>install</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>[model_overrides]</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">an agent whose model is not the model of its tier</td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>install</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>[model_variants]</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the <a href="./concepts#effort">effort</a> of every agent on one model: OpenCode&#39;s <code>variant:</code>, oh-my-pi&#39;s <code>thinking-level</code></td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>install</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>[variants]</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the effort of one agent or council seat, over the effort of its model</td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>install</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>[reasoning_effort]</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the effort of one agent or council seat, for a model that has no variant for it: OpenCode&#39;s <code>reasoningEffort:</code>, oh-my-pi&#39;s <code>thinking-level</code></td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>install</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>[councils]</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the copies of an agent on other models; one seat may add <code>verify = true</code>, and then it also judges each verify round</td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>install</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>[context_limits]</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the input tokens that a model of a built-in provider may hold: a provider entry in <code>opencode.jsonc</code>, <code>contextWindow</code> in oh-my-pi&#39;s <code>models.yml</code>. A provider of <code>providers.toml</code> stops <code>install</code> with exit status 2: its model holds the limit as <code>context</code></td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>install</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>[opencode]</code>, <code>[omp]</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the same sub-tables, such as <code>[omp.models]</code>; each key replaces the shared key for that frontend alone</td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>install</code></td></tr></tbody></table><p>OpenCode and oh-my-pi share every table but <code>[claude]</code>, so that their agents run on the same models.</p><p>A table that names a tier by an old name, <code>opus</code>, <code>sonnet</code> or <code>haiku</code>, stops <code>harness install</code> with exit status 2, an error, and the message names the rename. <a href="./architecture#the-neutral-vocabulary">architecture.md</a> shows how each frontend reads a tier.</p><h2 id="the-providers" tabindex="-1">The providers <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-providers" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The providers&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>providers.toml</code> beside the profile lists, once each, the model servers and accounts that are not built into OpenCode and oh-my-pi. <code>install</code> writes each provider into the <code>provider</code> object of OpenCode&#39;s <code>opencode.jsonc</code> and into oh-my-pi&#39;s <code>models.yml</code>. The file is optional. <code>examples/providers.toml</code> shows it. A table is one provider, named as <code>models.toml</code> names it in <code>provider/model</code>.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;">key</th><th style="text-align:left;">what it holds</th><th style="text-align:left;">OpenCode</th><th style="text-align:left;">oh-my-pi</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>api</code></td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>&quot;anthropic&quot;</code> or <code>&quot;openai-compatible&quot;</code></td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>npm</code>: <code>@ai-sdk/anthropic</code>, <code>@ai-sdk/openai-compatible</code></td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>api</code>: <code>anthropic-messages</code>, <code>openai-completions</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>url</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the base URL, with its <code>/v1</code></td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>options.baseURL</code></td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>baseUrl</code>, without the <code>/v1</code> for <code>&quot;anthropic&quot;</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>name</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the name that the frontend shows; optional</td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>name</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">—</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>key</code></td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>{env:VAR}</code> or <code>{file:PATH}</code>; optional. A literal key stops <code>install</code> with exit status 2</td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>options.apiKey</code>, as written</td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>apiKey</code>: <code>VAR</code>, or <code>!cat PATH</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>frontends</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the frontends that get the provider; optional, both by default</td><td style="text-align:left;"></td><td style="text-align:left;"></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>[&lt;name&gt;.models.&quot;&lt;id&gt;&quot;]</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">a model: <code>name</code>, <code>reasoning</code>, and <code>context</code> with <code>output</code>, each optional</td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>models.&lt;id&gt;</code>: <code>name</code>, <code>reasoning</code>, <code>limit</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">an item of <code>models</code>: <code>id</code>, <code>name</code>, <code>reasoning</code> (<code>false</code> when the model has none, so that oh-my-pi sends no effort, as OpenCode does), <code>contextWindow</code>, <code>maxTokens</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>effort</code> of a model</td><td style="text-align:left;">the reasoning effort that the model always gets, such as <code>&quot;none&quot;</code>; optional</td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>options.reasoningEffort</code></td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>reasoning = true</code>, and in <code>compat</code> a <code>reasoningEffortMap</code> that maps each thinking level to it and <code>reasoningDisableMode = &quot;none-effort&quot;</code>; an agent that turns thinking off sends <code>&quot;none&quot;</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>opencode</code>, <code>omp</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">a frontend&#39;s own keys, of a provider or of a model, merged over the rest</td><td style="text-align:left;">merged</td><td style="text-align:left;">merged</td></tr></tbody></table><p>A key that the table does not name stops <code>install</code> with exit status 2, and so does the profile key <code>opencode_providers</code> or <code>omp_providers</code>: move its providers into <code>providers.toml</code>.</p><h2 id="the-network-overlay" tabindex="-1">The network overlay <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-network-overlay" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The network overlay&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The settings template names no host. <code>settings/network.toml</code> holds the hosts of the harness, and the overlay <code>network.toml</code> beside the profile adds your own, such as the library resolver of your institution. The overlay is optional. <code>examples/network.toml</code> shows it. Both files have the same keys, and each key is optional in the overlay.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;">key</th><th style="text-align:left;">what it holds</th><th style="text-align:left;">what the install writes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>allow</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the hosts that the <code>WebFetch</code> tool and a sandboxed command can reach; write a host, never <code>*.X</code></td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>WebFetch(domain:X)</code> in <code>permissions.allow</code>; <code>X</code> in <code>sandbox.network.allowedDomains</code>, and <code>*.X</code> too when <code>X</code> is its own registrable domain</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>infrastructure</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the hosts that only a sandboxed command can reach, such as a package registry; <code>*.X</code> is allowed</td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>X</code> in <code>allowedDomains</code>, as written</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>deny</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the hosts that neither can reach; <code>*</code>, <code>*.X</code> and <code>X:port</code> are allowed</td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>X</code> in <code>sandbox.network.deniedDomains</code>; <code>WebFetch(domain:X)</code> in <code>permissions.deny</code>, except for an entry with a port</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>[deny_reasons]</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">a reason for a <code>deny</code> entry, keyed by the exact entry</td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>sandbox.network.deniedDomainReasons</code>, which Claude Code shows the model when the <a href="./concepts#sandbox">sandbox</a> refuses the host</td></tr></tbody></table><p><code>install</code>, <code>render</code> of the settings template and <code>settings</code> read both files. A <code>deny</code> entry of either file removes each <code>allow</code> and <code>infrastructure</code> host that it matches from the generated allow lists. A <code>deny</code> entry with a port leaves the host on the allow lists. The sandbox refuses a host that no list names, with no prompt, because the template sets <code>strictAllowlist</code>. A <code>WebFetch</code> of such a host is on no list either, so it asks in the default permission mode.</p><p>These cases stop the verb with exit status 2:</p><ul><li>an entry that is a URL;</li><li>an entry with a port outside <code>deny</code>;</li><li>a host in both <code>allow</code> and <code>deny</code> of one file;</li><li>a key that the table does not name;</li><li>the profile key <code>extra_domain</code>: move its hosts into the overlay&#39;s <code>allow</code>.</li></ul><p>The lists apply to Claude Code only. OpenCode and oh-my-pi have no network allowlist.</p>',36)])])}const m=t(l,[["render",s]]);export{p as __pageData,m as default};
